+          "quote": "Let $s\\ge1$ be a stage count, $A=(a_{ik})\\in\\mathbb R^{s\\times s}$, and $b,c\\in\\mathbb R^s$ satisfy $c_i=\\sum_{k=1}^s a_{ik}$ for each $i$. This is a (possibly implicit) Runge–Kutta tableau. On mesh cell $[t_j,t_{j+1}]$ with width $h_j$, a start state $X_{j,0}$ and piecewise-constant control $u_j$, define stage times $t_{j,i}=t_j+c_i h_j$ and the **collocation stage equations**\n$$X_{j,i}=X_{j,0}+h_j\\sum_{k=1}^s a_{ik}f(t_{j,k},X_{j,k},u_j),\\qquad i=1,\\ldots,s.$$\nThe equations are admissibly defined only when every stage time $t_{j,i}$ lies in the smooth-data domain $[0,T]$; otherwise this tableau/mesh pair is inadmissible and the stage data cannot be evaluated. A transcription procedure must report a stage-time-domain failure before querying $f$ or $L$. The corresponding endpoint equation is $X_{j+1,0}=X_{j,0}+h_j\\sum_{i=1}^s b_i f(t_{j,i},X_{j,i},u_j)$. In a finite transcription, an implicit tableau's coupled stage residuals are solved internally and the stage variables are eliminated from the outer NLP; the procedure returns the nonlinear stage-solve status and residuals. The quadrature contribution of the cell is $h_j\\sum_{i=1}^s b_i L(t_{j,i},X_{j,i},u_j)$. This definition asserts no stage-solution existence, uniqueness, order, stability, or error bound. If $A$ is strictly lower triangular, its stages are evaluated recursively by the explicit tableau procedure of [[def-explicit-runge-kutta-tableau-and-stage-map]].",
