+          "quote": "Fix a finite horizon $T>0$, state and control dimensions $n,m\\ge1$, and the real quotient space $L^2(0,T;\\mathbb R^m)$ of measurable controls modulo equality almost everywhere ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]). Let $A\\in L^\\infty(0,T;\\mathbb R^{n\\times n})$ and $B\\in L^\\infty(0,T;\\mathbb R^{n\\times m})$ be equivalence classes, let $x_0,x_T\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and let $x_d\\in L^2(0,T;\\mathbb R^n)$. Let $U_c\\subset\\mathbb R^m$ be nonempty, compact and convex. The pointwise control set is\n$$\n\\mathcal U_c:=\\{u\\in L^2(0,T;\\mathbb R^m):u(t)\\in U_c\\text{ for almost every }t\\}.\n$$\nLet $Q\\in L^\\infty(0,T;\\mathbb R^{n\\times n})$ and $R\\in L^\\infty(0,T;\\mathbb R^{m\\times m})$ be symmetric almost everywhere, with $Q(t)\\succeq0$ and $rI\\preceq R(t)\\preceq MI$ almost everywhere for constants $0<r\\le M<\\infty$. Let $P\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ be symmetric positive semidefinite. For each $u\\in\\mathcal U_c$, its state is the unique continuous solution of the Volterra equation\n$$\nx_u(t)=x_0+\\int_0^t\\big(A(s)x_u(s)+B(s)u(s)\\big)\\,ds.\n$$\nThe same state equation is defined for every $u\\in L^2(0,T;\\mathbb R^m)$, and the running and terminal cost on that ambient space is\n$$\nJ(u)=\\frac12\\int_0^T\\!\\left((x_u(t)-x_d(t))^TQ(t)(x_u(t)-x_d(t))+u(t)^TR(t)u(t)\\right)dt+\\frac12(x_u(T)-x_T)^TP(x_u(T)-x_T).\n$$\nThe constrained problem minimizes this ambient functional over $\\mathcal F$, where additional path or terminal constraints are represented by a nonempty convex weakly sequentially closed set $\\mathcal F\\subseteq\\mathcal U_c$; with no additional state constraints, $\\mathcal F=\\mathcal U_c$. “Weakly sequentially closed” means that every weakly convergent sequence in $\\mathcal F$ has its weak limit in $\\mathcal F$ ([[def-weak-convergence-of-nets-and-sequences]]). The definition asserts no optimizer or adjoint existence; the state-map well-definedness is supplied by [[lem-ctrl1-measurable-li